WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:01.450
Hello, everybody.

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:02.440
I'm Jordan.

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.540
Thank you so much
for inviting me here.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.880
I'm excited to share
with you our Google Cloud

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:14.600
solution that we started looking
into almost a year ago today.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.440
So to give you just a brief
history-- so I'll try and speed

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.025
through this as
quickly as I can--

00:00:19.025 --> 00:00:21.110
I work for a company
called Rodeo FX that

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.720
was started in 2006.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.220
The company in 2006
was basically matte

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.560
painting and compositing.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.336
So you can think
Photoshop, a lot

00:00:30.336 --> 00:00:31.710
of different layers
in Photoshop,

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.950
and then you can
think integrating it

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.200
into a live plate environment.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.700
Pretty cool.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.616
At this time, I was actually
working for Sony Pictures

00:00:41.616 --> 00:00:42.240
at Image Works.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.610
And although I'm a
software engineer,

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.790
I got a job working
the graveyard shift

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.740
on Polar Express.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.970
I was a render wrangler.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:56.730
So effectively, my job was
managing the render farm,

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.230
managing the storage, and making
sure it all worked correctly.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:03.251
If you guys could have told
me 10 or 11 years later

00:01:03.251 --> 00:01:05.500
I would be sitting up here
talking to you about render

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.240
farm, storage, and
making it work correctly,

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.562
I don't even know what to say.

00:01:11.562 --> 00:01:14.750
So I'm quite surprised.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:21.230
So then I moved from Sony Los
Angeles to lovely Montreal.

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:26.130
I moved from a company that was
massive with a massive pipeline

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.270
and a massive infrastructure
to a company that

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:32.419
was about yay size when I got
there, maybe about 30 people.

00:01:32.419 --> 00:01:33.585
We all fit into a stairwell.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:40.580
And effectively I was tasked
with the filling in the rest

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:43.600
of the pipeline, being
able to bring Rodeo

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.770
to a company that
would be much larger,

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:48.690
that could handle all
aspects of visual effects.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:52.320
And as of today, we are growing.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.610
We are actually 230 people.

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.760
We are stretched across three
different studios in Montreal,

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.220
Los Angeles, and Quebec city.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:05.980
And as described, we actually
won our very first Emmy award

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:09.350
for Game of Thrones, which
we were really excited about.

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:10.050
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:10.800
Thank you so much.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.380
And then on top of that, we
got a Visual Effects Award

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.220
for our work on "Birdman,"
which we were incredibly

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.460
excited about as well.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:24.130
So we are definitely originally
a tiny little boutique studio,

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:27.190
but as you can see,
we are moving our way

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:30.700
up in the industry.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.570
That said, one of the things
that was really important

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:37.670
was, especially a studio that
was maybe 15 to 30 people,

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.190
is we wanted to be nimble.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.520
We wanted to sort of retain
the qualities that made

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.170
us successful in the beginning.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.870
So this nimbleness,
the ability to be

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:48.960
flexible the ability
to be inventive,

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.250
we didn't want to be
constrained to any one thing.

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:55.350
We wanted to be able
to make all things work

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.720
and whatever the
solution could be,

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.570
we were going to go with the
one that was the most amazing

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:03.370
and the best at that
time in order to bring

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:07.720
about the most beautiful shot.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:08.960
That's pretty cool.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:12.650
So this is Rodeo FX today.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:14.150
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:19.130
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:40.322 --> 00:06:42.314
[END PLAYBACK]

00:06:42.314 --> 00:06:44.831
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:44.831 --> 00:06:46.206
Thank you.

00:06:46.206 --> 00:06:47.940
So that's really cool.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.720
But my position is
to make sure that I'm

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.320
looking ahead in the future.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:55.330
And so, I'm really
happy that we're

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:57.030
able to present a
reel like that today,

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:00.500
but of course, I need to
be constantly looking ahead

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:03.600
and trying to find the best
tools and the best workflow

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.920
to allow us to continue
to not only produce

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:10.170
these types of images, but also
to produce even better ones.

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:17.000
And of course, the focus for
us has always been the same--

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:20.710
produce the most beautiful
image that we can.

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:24.240
And we're never
satisfied until we do.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.580
But beautiful shots take
a lot of time to render.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:31.240
And unfortunately,
you can through CPUs,

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:35.440
you can throw storage, you
control everything at it,

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:38.190
but time is never your ally.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:42.520
And as we wrapped up last
year around this time for Game

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.620
of Thrones, I
needed to look ahead

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.420
to figure out how I was
going to be able to support

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:49.090
a lot of different productions.

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:52.840
I needed to be able to come up
with my own emergency button.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:55.230
And that was actually
what brings me

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:58.620
to my presentation
today-- the way

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:01.646
in which I ended up building--
or we ended up building,

00:08:01.646 --> 00:08:03.520
excuse me, because
there's a ton of engineers

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:07.947
help me on to do this we ended
up building our Google Cloud

00:08:07.947 --> 00:08:09.030
proof of concept solution.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:15.130
So the first thing
was transparency.

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:17.220
Artists are very fickle.

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:20.669
They do not like change.

00:08:20.669 --> 00:08:22.460
And they particularly
don't care if they're

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:24.980
rendering in the cloud or
rendering locally or rendering

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:26.100
on their machine.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:30.600
They just don't care to know
what happens under the hood.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:33.435
So whatever we did,
whatever solution we chose,

00:08:33.435 --> 00:08:35.559
it had to be something that
was more or less hidden

00:08:35.559 --> 00:08:38.370
from the artist and
hidden from production.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:40.780
On top of that, scalable.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:43.340
Obviously, we are
a small studio,

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:46.930
so the ability to be able to
scale, to be able to burst,

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:50.150
to be able to handle whatever
emergencies are coming up

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:53.430
at that time is super important.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:54.870
Easy integration and management.

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.589
We have built out our
own pipeline-- I mean,

00:08:57.589 --> 00:08:59.130
obviously, we have
our own pipeline--

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:02.520
and we've integrated
it enormously

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:05.160
with products like Shotgun
and with products like Cube.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:09.630
And we really had no intention
of tossing that all away

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.520
and trying to adopt
something else in parallel

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.160
or trying to completely
reinvent the wheel.

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:20.040
We wanted to use what we had,
we just wanted it to just work.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.440
Obviously, it
needed to be secure.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.360
You can just take a
look at the Sony hack,

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:27.800
you could take a
look at Expendables.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:30.850
It's a common theme
in our industry.

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:32.830
And it has to be
price-conscious.

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:36.600
We use our render farm seven
days a week, 24 hours a day.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:39.600
So those needs need
to be able to fit

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:44.320
and not completely put
us out of business.

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:46.670
So the first thing
I did was I enlisted

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:51.250
one of our engineers, Alan, and
I called him a Cloud Pipeline

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:54.890
TD, and he seemed to be
cool with that title.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.840
And so, he ended up sort
of helping me build out

00:09:57.840 --> 00:09:59.810
this proof of concept.

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:03.100
The first idea that was the
not invented here strategy.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:07.760
I went and took a look at a
lot of common turn-key options.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.380
And unfortunately, nothing
really met our needs.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:15.710
So we ended up going with the
build it yourself strategy.

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:20.750
And the same vein, we
started looking at all

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:22.370
the different options there.

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:25.290
And what became sort
of a common theme was,

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:26.830
Google ended up
coming out, checking

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:28.670
all of the boxes for us.

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:31.440
So once we had sort of decided
on where we were going to go,

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:34.260
now we needed to start
building our solution.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.580
So, this is sort
of our prototype.

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:39.800
We started with a single machine
which just one NFS share.

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:42.200
And it could handle
a dozen slaves,

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.360
and we were super
happy about that.

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:48.380
But it failed.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.030
As soon as we went
above about a dozen,

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:52.850
all of a sudden,
a frame that would

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:56.660
take maybe 20 minutes suddenly
was taking an hour and a half.

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:58.680
And we had angry artists.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:02.430
So we needed to go
back and rethink.

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:06.292
So then we ended up
choosing GlusterFS.

00:11:06.292 --> 00:11:07.250
And it was open source.

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:12.680
And so just to give you a
sense of how things grow,

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:18.730
September 22, 2014, we went
with a 9 terabyte usable stores.

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:20.000
We thought, that's amazing.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:21.690
That will be more than enough.

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:23.300
And it wasn't enough.

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:27.130
We toppled that thing
really, really quickly.

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:29.630
I think at that time we
were working on Sunday night

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:32.650
football, the opening
for Sunday night football

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.490
and the Super Bowl, and
it was just killing us.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:38.850
So we ended up by increasing
the storage space.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:43.780
We went to 18 terabytes,
and again, to 30 terabytes.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:49.010
As you can see, we just keep
needing more and more storage,

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:50.240
and we were using it.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:53.270
Finally toppled out at 48.

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:55.610
And we could scale the storage.

00:11:55.610 --> 00:11:57.030
And we were like, fantastic.

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:58.080
We've totally fixed it.

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:02.240
If I render locally at Rodeo,
if I render on the cloud,

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:05.660
we're getting equivalent
times, artists are happy,

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:08.950
and we have a little
bit more flexibility.

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:13.590
But the configuration on the
cloud was really not ideal.

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:17.170
So we needed to go back
and again, rethink that.

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:20.150
Initially, we were just using
a very kind of simple VM.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:22.750
And what we would do is update
it and then push it out.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:25.610
But ultimately, that also
became a really, really,

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:26.860
really hard thing to manage.

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:28.859
So we needed to sort of
go back and think, well,

00:12:28.859 --> 00:12:31.620
what's more flexible,
what's more granular?

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:34.640
So we started using Salt. And
like I said, I'm providing you

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:35.576
with a solution.

00:12:35.576 --> 00:12:38.820
I'm not revealing any
secret strategies here,

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:40.920
Rodeo doesn't work
with secret strategies.

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:43.540
We try and work with
whatever is available,

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:47.200
whatever is out there, we
think that's the easiest

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:49.130
way to get from A to B.

00:12:49.130 --> 00:12:52.820
So we use Salt-- open source,
free, it's actually quite good.

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:54.530
So we've got our
Salt master, we've

00:12:54.530 --> 00:12:58.210
got all of our Salt minions,
and the Salt minions then

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.390
ask the Salt master for
their configuration,

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:02.280
and we can manage
these configurations

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:03.330
in one central spot.

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:06.720
And guess what-- it works and
it actually works pretty well.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:09.170
As soon as an
instance starts up,

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:11.700
it makes sure that it
has everything it needs.

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:15.070
As soon as it starts up, it
has everything it needs-- boom,

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:18.180
Cube, which is our
render tool, immediately

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:21.790
starts up and starts accepting
jobs and starts rendering.

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:26.950
And then as soon as we did that,
our Salt master accidentally

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:29.050
went down and we
realized, we probably

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:32.130
could use more than
one to have redundancy.

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:35.200
So we ended up going with
a multi-master setup,

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:37.150
and that ended up working
really well as well.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:42.290
The other issue
that we had was just

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:47.440
IO from Montreal to
the Google Cloud.

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:51.630
Montreal's a great city, but
its connectivity to Google

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:55.450
is a little shaky at best.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:57.970
And of course, the first
thing we used was rsync.

00:13:57.970 --> 00:14:02.330
And rsync is not awesome.

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:05.780
And it was topping out at
300 megabits per second,

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:09.770
so it just it simply
wasn't meeting our needs.

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:13.850
So then we found a project
by the guys who smash atoms

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:16.520
together, an open source
project called FDT,

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:18.020
so made by the CERN lab.

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:20.230
And suddenly we were
able to get speeds

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:23.420
that were huge-- one gigabit.

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:29.830
So huge, in fact, that I started
taking down Rodeo FX anytime

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:31.060
I started pushing data up.

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:34.690
So we ended up
solving that problem.

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:35.760
Everything worked great.

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:37.330
We completely have
our solution now.

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:39.370
We've solved the
disk storage, we've

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:43.200
solved the ability to
move data back and forth.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:45.670
So everything's fantastic.

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:50.320
So effectively, we've sort of
built our own little pipeline.

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:54.420
And the idea is, when anybody,
especially with a company

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:57.650
like Rodeo FX,
where typically we

00:14:57.650 --> 00:15:01.660
are looking to render locally
and live at Rodeo as much as we

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:04.710
can, but when it
gets too out of hand,

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:06.820
we need to be able to
press the emergency button.

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:11.150
And that's the whole point of
our solution into Google Cloud.

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:18.390
So Carrie Underwood, the Super
Bowl-- boom, emergency button.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:20.910
Summons the Gluster
storage pool,

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:23.220
suddenly we have our storage.

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:26.940
The artists then tick,
yeah, use the Google Cloud.

00:15:26.940 --> 00:15:28.840
They don't care.

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:33.200
Then, cleverly enough,
it goes back to Shotgun.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:35.325
Shotgun all of a
sudden goes, great,

00:15:35.325 --> 00:15:36.700
do you want to
render this scene?

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:38.330
Well, let me go and
take a look using

00:15:38.330 --> 00:15:43.580
the Shotgun toolkit--
amazing, amazing, awesome API.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:47.000
It says great, you
need all of this stuff.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:48.770
Bam, thank you, Shotgun.

00:15:48.770 --> 00:15:55.090
And then we use the CERN lab
FDT to upload everything.

00:15:55.090 --> 00:15:58.660
The VMs are booted,
the VMs are configured,

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:03.140
and all of a sudden we start
rendering Arnold, Houdini,

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:04.160
whatever you want.

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:06.540
And everybody is happy.

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:08.100
And then once the
rendering is done,

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:11.090
we use the same
product from CERN

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:14.120
to pull it back down to Rodeo.

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:16.390
Not terribly complicated.

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:22.030
And like I said, just a
lot of engineering, time,

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:25.770
and a lot of failures to be
able to get to a solution that

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:27.200
ultimately ends up working.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:28.940
And so we're quite
happy and quite

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:30.175
pleased with what we've got.

00:16:30.175 --> 00:16:32.800
And effectively, we were able to
transform our emergency button

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:39.379
into a Google emergency
button and then turn it off,

00:16:39.379 --> 00:16:41.420
because we only need it
when we actually need it.

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:45.000
But it's always sort
of there, ever-present.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.160
And it's exactly
the type of stuff

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:50.740
that when we need to
call upon it again,

